Solving Stream Api functions:

Instead of just jumping to random functions, we should follow: Data → transform → collect and At every line, ask: “What is the type of the stream RIGHT NOW?”

Step 1: Understand the INPUT clearly
	Is it List<T>?
Is it Map<K, V>?
Is it array?
Step 2: Decide the OUTPUT (VERY IMPORTANT)
	Single value? (int, String)
Collection? (List<T>, Set<T>)
Map? (Map<K, V>)
Optional?
Step 3: Convert to stream (only ONCE)
	list.stream()
map.entrySet().stream()
Arrays.stream(arr)
Step 4: Apply transformations (0–N times)

Method
Purpose
filter
Remove elements
map
Transform
flatMap
Flatten
distinct
Remove duplicates
sorted
Sort

Step 5: Apply terminal operation (ONLY ONE)

Goal
Terminal op
Collection
collect()
Single value
findFirst(), reduce()
Boolean
anyMatch()
Count
count()

Step 6: Verify logic WITHOUT stream (IMPORTANT)
Before coding, ask: “How would I solve this with a loop?”
If you can’t do it with a loop, Stream won’t save you.

Common Stream Patterns (MUST PRACTICE)

Pattern 1️: Filter → Map → Collect
List<String> names = users.stream()
    .filter(u -> u.getAge() > 18)
    .map(User::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

Pattern 2️: Grouping
Map<String, List<Employee>> map =
    employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getDept));

Pattern 3️: Grouping + Aggregation
Map<String, Integer> sumByDept =
    employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
            Employee::getDept,
            Collectors.summingInt(Employee::getSalary)
        ));

Pattern 4️: Max / Min
employees.stream()
    .max(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getSalary));

Pattern 5️: Map → EntrySet → Stream
map.entrySet().stream()
   .filter(e -> e.getValue() > 100)
   .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
   .collect(Collectors.toList());

Comparator Questions
For any comparator question, separate these two concerns:
WHO should be considered? → filtering
HOW should they be ordered? → comparator

For eg. There is a question, we are having an employee class where we have age and marks, we have to sort according to the marks whose age > 30.

Step-by-Step Thinking (Memorize this flow)

Step 1️: Understand the data
class Employee {
    private int age;
    private int marks;

    // constructor

    public int getAge(){
    	return age;
    }

    public int getMarks(){
    	return marks;
    }

   // setters
}
Input: List<Employee>

Step 2️: Apply condition (age > 30)
This is NOT comparator logic — this is filtering.

Step 3️: Decide sorting logic
Sort by: marks (ascending or descending — clarify)

Answer: List<Employee> sortedList = list.stream()
					.filter(s -> s.getAge() > 30)
					.sorted(collectors.collectingInt(Employee:getMarks))
					.collect(Collectors.toList());
